\begin_layout Enumerate
Write down the system 2SLS estimator if the instrument matrix is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
z_{i}=\left(\begin{array}{c}
z_{i1}^{\prime}\\
\vdots\\
z_{iT}^{\prime}
\end{array}\right)_{T\times L}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Show that this estimator is a pooled 2SLS estimator.
 That is, it is the estimator obtained by 2SLS estimation of (1) using instrumen
ts 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{it}$
\end_inset

, pooled across all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
What is the rank condition for the pooled 2SLS estimator?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Without further assumptions, show how to estimate the asymptotic variance
 of the pooled 2SLS estimator.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Without further assumptions, how would you estimate the optimal weighting
 matrix?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Show that the assumptions
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(u_{it}|z_{it},u_{i,t-1},z_{i,t-1},\ldots,u_{i1},z_{i1})=0,\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(u_{it}^{2}|z_{it})=\sigma^{2},\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

imply that the usual standard errors and test statistics reported from the
 pooled 2SLS estimation are valid.
 These assumptions make implementing 2SLS for panel data very simple.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
What estimator would you use under condition (13) but where we relax condition
 (14) to 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{it}^{2}|z_{it})=E(u_{it}^{2})\equiv\sigma_{t}^{2}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

? This approach will involve an initial pooled 2SLS estimation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Suppose you choose the instrument matrix as 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}=\mbox{diag}(z_{i1}^{\prime},\ldots,z_{iT}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 Show that the system 2SLS estimator can be obtained as follows.
 (1) For each 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, regress 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{it}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

, and obtain the fitted values, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{it}$
\end_inset

.
 (2) Compute the pooled IV estimator using 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{it}$
\end_inset

 as IVs for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
What is the most efficient way to use the moment conditions 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{it}u_{it})=0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Solution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*

\series bold
Part a.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The system 2SLS estimator is just the system GMM estimator that uses
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{W}=\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}\right)^{-1}
\]

\end_inset

as its weighting matrix.
 Thus, after canceling 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

, the system 2SLS estimator is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\beta}_{S2SLS}=\left[\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}x_{i}\right)\right]^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}y_{i}\right)
\]

\end_inset

When 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 has the form of (11), the weighting matrix becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{W}=\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}z_{it}z_{it}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}
\]

\end_inset

and the estimator can be rewritten as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\hat{\beta} & = & \left[\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}x_{it}z_{it}^{\prime}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}z_{it}z_{it}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}z_{it}x_{it}^{\prime}\right)\right]^{-1}\\
 &  & \times\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}x_{it}z_{it}^{\prime}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}z_{it}z_{it}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}z_{it}y_{it}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

which is the pooled 2SLS estimator.
 The pooled 2SLS estimator treats the stacked panel data as a long cross
 section and performs 2SLS, i.e., it pools across time in estimating the reduced
 from.
 That is, the first-stage fitted values, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{it}$
\end_inset

, are obtained from the pooled regression 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{it}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,N$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*

\series bold
Part b.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the pooled 2SLS estimator to be consistent, the following two conditions
 must be satisfied: 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{rank}\ \left[E(z_{i}^{\prime}x_{i})\right]=\mbox{rank}\left[E\left(\sum_{t=1}^{T}z_{it}x_{it}^{\prime}\right)\right]=K$
\end_inset

 and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{W}\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{p}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}W=\left[E(z_{i}^{\prime}z_{i})\right]^{-1}\ \mbox{as}\ N\rightarrow\infty
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $W$
\end_inset

 is a nonrandom, symmetric, and 
\begin_inset Formula $L\times L$
\end_inset

 positive definite matrix.
 So, the second condition requires that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}^{\prime}z_{i})$
\end_inset

 exist and be nonsingular, so we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{rank}\ \left[E(z_{i}^{\prime}z_{i})\right]=\mbox{rank}\ \left[E\left(\sum_{t=1}^{T}z_{it}z_{it}^{\prime}\right)\right]=L$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Part c.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Substituting 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{it}=x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+u_{it}$
\end_inset

 in the pooled 2SLS estimator and stacking over 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\left(\hat{\beta}_{P2SLS}-\beta\right) & = & \left[\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}x_{i}\right)\right]^{-1}\\
 &  & \times\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}\right)\\
\sqrt{N}\left(\hat{\beta}_{P2SLS}-\beta\right) & = & \left[S_{xz}\hat{W}S_{zx}\right]^{-1}S_{xz}\hat{W}\left({\displaystyle N^{-\frac{1}{2}}\sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{xz}=N^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{N}x_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{zx}=N^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{N}z_{i}^{\prime}x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Because 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}^{\prime}u_{i})=0$
\end_inset

, the CLT implies that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle N^{-\frac{1}{2}}\sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{d}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}N\left(0,B\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $B\equiv E(z_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}z_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 In particular, 
\begin_inset Formula $ $
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $N^{-\frac{1}{2}}\sum_{i=1}^{N}z_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}=O_{p}(1)$
\end_inset

.
 But, by the law of large numbers,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left[S_{xz}\hat{W}S_{zx}\right]^{-1}S_{xz}\hat{W}=\left[\Sigma_{xz}W\Sigma_{zx}\right]^{-1}\Sigma_{xz}W+o_{p}(1)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{xz}=E(x_{i}^{\prime}z_{i})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{zx}=E(z_{i}^{\prime}x_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 So, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\sqrt{N}\left(\hat{\beta}_{P2SLS}-\beta\right) & = & \left[\Sigma_{xz}W\Sigma_{zx}\right]^{-1}\Sigma_{xz}W\left(N^{-\frac{1}{2}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}\right)\nonumber \\
 &  & +\left(\left[S_{xz}\hat{W}S_{zx}\right]^{-1}S_{xz}\hat{W}-\left[\Sigma_{xz}W\Sigma_{zx}\right]^{-1}\Sigma_{xz}W\right)\left(N^{-\frac{1}{2}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}\right)\nonumber \\
 & = & \left[\Sigma_{xz}W\Sigma_{zx}\right]^{-1}\Sigma_{xz}W\left(N^{-\frac{1}{2}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}\right)+o_{p}(1)\cdot O_{p}(1)\nonumber \\
 & = & \left[\Sigma_{xz}W\Sigma_{zx}\right]^{-1}\Sigma_{xz}W\left(N^{-\frac{1}{2}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}\right)+o_{p}(1)
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Thus, by the CLT, equations (16) and (17) and the asymptotic equivalence
 lemma imply
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sqrt{N}\left(\hat{\beta}_{P2LS}-\beta\right)\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{d}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}N\left(0,ABA^{\prime}\right)
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $A\equiv\left[\Sigma_{xz}W\Sigma_{zx}\right]^{-1}\Sigma_{xz}W$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, the asymptotic variance of the pooled 2SLS estimator is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\beta}_{P2SLS})=N^{-1}\cdot ABA^{\prime}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Therefore, the asymptotic variance is consistently estimated by 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\beta}_{P2SLS})}=N^{-1}\cdot\hat{A}\hat{B}\hat{A}^{\prime}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{A}\equiv\left[S_{xz}\hat{W}S_{zx}\right]^{-1}S_{xz}\hat{W}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{B}\equiv N^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{N}z_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Equivalently, equation (19) can also be written as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\beta}_{P2SLS})}=C\left(\sum_{i=1}^{N}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}\sum_{s=1}^{T}u_{it}u_{is}z_{it}z_{is}^{\prime}\right)C^{\prime}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $C\equiv\left[\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}x_{it}z_{it}^{\prime}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}z_{it}z_{it}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}z_{it}x_{it}^{\prime}\right)\right]^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}x_{it}z_{it}^{\prime}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}z_{it}z_{it}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Part d.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Without further assumptions, the pooled 2SLS estimator is not necessarily
 the asymptotically efficient estimator.
 The most efficient GMM estimator is the GMM estimator where the weighting
 matrix is chosen to be
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
W & = & \left[E(z_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}z_{i})\right]^{-1}\\
 & = & \left[E\left(\sum_{t=1}^{T}\sum_{s=1}^{T}u_{it}u_{is}z_{it}z_{is}^{\prime}\right)\right]^{-1}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $W$
\end_inset

 is known as the optimal weighting matrix since it produces the GMM estimator
 with the smallest asymptotic variance.
 A consistent estimator of this weighting matrix is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\hat{W} & = & \left[N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}\right]^{-1}\\
 & = & \left[N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}{\displaystyle \sum_{s=1}^{T}}u_{it}u_{is}z_{it}z_{is}^{\prime}\right]^{-1}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Part e.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Asumptions (13) and (14) imply that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(z_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}z_{i})=E(z_{i}^{\prime}\Omega z_{i})
\]

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime})\equiv\Omega=\sigma^{2}I_{T}
\]

\end_inset

Thus, system homoskedasticity holds, and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 has a spherical second moment.
 Then, the optimal weighting matrix becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
W=\left[\sigma^{2}E(z_{i}^{\prime}z_{i})\right]^{-1}=\left[\sigma^{2}\Sigma_{zz}\right]^{-1}
\]

\end_inset

which is consistently estimated by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{W}=\left[\hat{\sigma}^{2}\frac{1}{N}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}\right]^{-1}=\left[\hat{\sigma}^{2}S_{zz}\right]^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Plugging equation (21) into the expression for the GMM estimator, along
 with canceling out 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}^{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

, we obtain the pooled 2SLS estimator.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\hat{\beta} & = & \left[\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}x_{i}\right)\right]^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}y_{i}\right)\\
 & = & \left[\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}x_{it}z_{it}^{\prime}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}z_{it}z_{it}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}z_{it}x_{it}^{\prime}\right)\right]^{-1}\\
 &  & \times\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}x_{it}z_{it}^{\prime}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}z_{it}z_{it}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}z_{it}y_{it}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

So, under Assumptions (13) and (14), the efficient GMM estimator that uses
 (21) as its optimal weighting matrix is equivalent to the pooled 2SLS estimator
, which is the GMM estimator that uses
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{W}=\left[N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}\right]^{-1}
\]

\end_inset

as its weighting matrix.
 Also, under system homoskedasticity, the asymptotic variance of the pooled
 2SLS estimator becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\beta}_{P2SLS}) & = & N^{-1}\cdot ABA^{\prime}\\
 & = & N^{-1}\cdot\left[\Sigma_{xz}W\Sigma_{zx}\right]^{-1}\Sigma_{xz}WE(z_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}z_{i})W\Sigma_{zx}\left[\Sigma_{xz}W\Sigma_{zx}\right]^{-1}\\
 & = & N^{-1}\cdot\left[\Sigma_{xz}\left(\sigma^{2}\Sigma_{zz}\right)^{-1}\Sigma_{zx}\right]^{-1}\Sigma_{xz}\left(\sigma^{2}\Sigma_{zz}\right)^{-1}\Sigma_{zx}\left[\Sigma_{xz}\left(\sigma^{2}\Sigma_{zz}\right)^{-1}\Sigma_{zx}\right]^{-1}\\
 & = & N^{-1}\cdot\sigma^{2}\left[\Sigma_{xz}(\Sigma_{zz})^{-1}\Sigma_{zx}\right]^{-1}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Hence, the asymptotic variance is consistently estimated by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\beta}_{P2SLS})}=N^{-1}\cdot\hat{\sigma}^{2}\left[S_{xz}(S_{zz})^{-1}S_{zx}\right]^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\sigma}^{2}\equiv N^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\hat{u}_{it}^{2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}_{it}=y_{it}-x_{it}^{\prime}\hat{\beta}_{P2SLS}$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $ $
\end_inset

Equivalently, equation (22) can be written as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\widehat{\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\beta}_{P2SLS})}=\hat{\sigma}^{2}\left[\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}x_{it}z_{it}^{\prime}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}z_{it}z_{it}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}z_{it}x_{it}^{\prime}\right)\right]^{-1}
\]

\end_inset

Therefore, assumptions (13) and (14) imply that the usual standard errors
 and test statistics reported from the pooled 2SLS estimation are valid.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Part f.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this part, we still maintain condition (13), but condition (14) has been
 relaxed to
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(u_{it}^{2}|z_{it})=E(u_{it}^{2})\equiv\sigma_{T}^{2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Assumptions (13) and (24) imply that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(z_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}z_{i})=E(z_{i}^{\prime}\Omega z_{i})
\]

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime})\equiv\Omega=\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
\sigma_{1}^{2} &  & 0\\
 & \ddots\\
0 &  & \sigma_{T}^{2}
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset

So, system homoskedasticity still holds, but 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 no longer has a sperical second moment.
 Consequently, the pooled 2SLS estimator is no longer the asymptotically
 efficient GMM estimator.
 Since system homoskedasticity still holds, then by Theorem 8.4, the 3SLS
 estimator is an optimal GMM estimator.
 The GMM 3SLS estimator is a GMM estimator that uses
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{W}=\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}z_{i}\right)^{-1}=\left[N^{-1}\cdot Z^{\prime}\left(I_{N}\otimes\hat{\Omega}\right)Z\right]^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

as its particular weighting matrix.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\check{u}_{i}=y_{i}-x_{i}\check{\beta}$
\end_inset

 be the residuals from an initial P2SLS estimator.
 It can be shown that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\Omega}\equiv N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\check{u}_{i}\check{u}_{i}^{\prime}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

is a consistent estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

.
 Plugging equation (25) into the equation for the GMM estimator and canceling
 out 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 gives the 3SLS estimator
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\hat{\beta} & = & \left[\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}z_{i}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}x_{i}\right)\right]^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}z_{i}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}y_{i}\right)\\
 & = & \left[X^{\prime}Z\left\{ Z^{\prime}\left(I_{N}\otimes\hat{\Omega}\right)Z\right\} ^{-1}Z^{\prime}X\right]^{-1}X^{\prime}Z\left\{ Z^{\prime}\left(I_{N}\otimes\hat{\Omega}\right)Z\right\} ^{-1}Z^{\prime}Y
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Part g.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this part, we have chosen the instrument matrix as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
z_{i}=\mbox{diag}(z_{i1}^{\prime},\ldots,z_{iT}^{\prime})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and we choose the weighting matrix to be
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{W}=\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Plugging equation (28) into the equation for the GMM estimator and cancel
 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 everywhere, we get the S2LS estimator
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\beta}_{2SLS}=\left[\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}x_{i}\right)\right]^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}y_{i}\right)
\]

\end_inset

Straightforward partitioned multiplication shows that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}z_{i} & = & \left[\begin{array}{ccc}
{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i1}z_{i1}^{\prime}\\
 & \ddots\\
 &  & {\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{iT}z_{iT}^{\prime}
\end{array}\right]\\
\sum_{i=1}^{N}z_{i}^{\prime}x_{i} & = & \left[\begin{array}{c}
{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i1}x_{i1}^{\prime}\\
\vdots\\
{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{iT}x_{iT}^{\prime}
\end{array}\right]\\
{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}y_{i} & = & \left[\begin{array}{c}
{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i1}y_{i1}\\
\vdots\\
{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{iT}y_{iT}
\end{array}\right]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Now, block partitioned inversion and multiplication given
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}x_{i}\right) & = & {\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{it}z_{it}^{\prime}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{it}z_{it}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{it}x_{it}^{\prime}\right)\\
 & = & {\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\hat{x}_{it}x_{it}^{\prime}\\
 & = & {\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}\hat{x}_{it}x_{it}^{\prime}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{it}^{\prime}=z_{it}^{\prime}\hat{\Pi}_{t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\Pi}_{t}=\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}z_{it}z_{it}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}z_{it}x_{it}^{\prime}\right)$
\end_inset

 is the matrix of first-stage regression coefficients for time period 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

.
 Similarly,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}y_{i}\right)={\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}\hat{x}_{it}^{\prime}y_{it}
\]

\end_inset

Putting these results together gives
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\beta}_{S2SLS}=\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}\hat{x}_{it}x_{it}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}\hat{x}_{it}^{\prime}y_{it}\right)
\]

\end_inset

Now, suppose we regress 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{it}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

, and obtain the fitted values, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{it}^{\prime}=z_{it}^{\prime}\hat{\Pi}_{t}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\Pi}_{t}=\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}z_{it}z_{it}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}z_{it}x_{it}^{\prime}\right)$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{t}\times K$
\end_inset

.
 Then, we compute the pooled IV estimator using 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{it}$
\end_inset

 as IVs for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\beta}_{IV}=\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\hat{x}_{i}^{\prime}x_{i}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\hat{x}_{i}^{\prime}y_{i}\right)
\]

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\hat{x}_{i} & = & \left[\begin{array}{c}
\hat{x}_{i1}^{\prime}\\
\vdots\\
\hat{x}_{iT}^{\prime}
\end{array}\right]\\
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Therefore, the S2SLS estimator can be obtained by finding the fitted values
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{it}$
\end_inset

 for each time period, and then calculate 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 as the pooled IV estimator, listing 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{it}$
\end_inset

 as instruments.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Part h.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The efficeint estimator that uses only the moments 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{it}u_{it})=0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

 is the generalized method of moments (GMM) estimator with an optimal weighting
 matrix.
 The choice of instrument matrix (12) means we are only using the moment
 conditions aggregated across time, 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{t=1}^{T}E(z_{it}u_{it})=0$
\end_inset

.
 The matrix of instruments should be as in as in (27).
 The system 2SLS estimator can be used in the first step to obtain the optimal
 weighting matrix.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Problem 10.10
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An unobserved effects model explaning current murder rates in terms of the
 number of executions in the last three years is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{\emph{mrdrte}}_{it}=\theta_{t}+\beta_{1}\mbox{\emph{exec}}_{it}+\beta_{2}\mbox{\emph{unem}}_{it}+c_{i}+u_{it}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{mrdrte}}_{it}$
\end_inset

 is the number of murders in state 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 during year 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, per 10,000 people; 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{exec}}_{it}$
\end_inset

 is the total number of executions for the current and prior two years;
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{unem}}_{it}$
\end_inset

 is the current unemployment rate, included as a control.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Using the data in MURDER.RAW, estiamte this model by FD.
 Notice that you should allow different year intercepts.
 Test the errors in the FD equation for serial correlation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Estiamte the model by FE.
 Are there any important differences from the FD estimates? What about in
 their fully robust standard errors?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Under what circumstances would 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{exec}}_{it}$
\end_inset

 not be strictly exogenous (conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

)?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Solution
\end_layout

