\begin_layout Standard
The main difference in estimates from the two regressions is significance
 of coefficient of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{exec}}_{it}$
\end_inset

.
 For FD estimation, the coefficient of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{exec}}_{it}$
\end_inset

 is -0.1151 with a p-value of 0.0029, and for FE estimation, the coefficient
 is -0.1383 with a p-value of 0.081.
 Hence, at the 5% level, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{exec}}_{it}$
\end_inset

 is significant for FD estimation and insignificant for FE estimation.
 Although the coefficients of all the other variables are (slightly) different
 for FD and FE estimations, there is no change in significance of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{unem}}_{it}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{d90}}_{t}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{d93}}_{t}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Part c.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are a few circumstances that would cause 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{exec}}_{it}$
\end_inset

 not to be strictly exogenous (conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

).
 One case is enacting or revoking legislation concerning the punishment
 of execution.
 Another would be the party affiliation of the leadership governing the
 state.
 States that lean conservative are more likely to have the death penalty,
 while many liberal states have done away with capital punishment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Problem 11.16
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consider a Hausman and Taylor type model with a single time-constant explanatory
 variable
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather}
y_{it}=\gamma w_{i}+x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}+u_{it}\\
E(u_{it}|w_{i},x_{i},c_{i})=0,\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{gather}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of time-varying explanatory variables.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
If we are interested only in estimating 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

, how should we proceed, without making additional assumptions (other than
 a standard rank assumption)?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

 be a time-constant proxy variable for 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 in the sense that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(c_{i}|r_{i},w_{i},x_{i})=E(c_{i}|r_{i},x_{i})=\delta_{0}+\delta_{1}r_{i}+\bar{x}_{i}\delta_{2}
\]

\end_inset

The key assumption is that, once we condition on 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

 is not partially related to 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Assuming the standard proxy variable redundancy assumption 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{it}|w_{i},x_{i},c_{i},r_{i})=0$
\end_inset

, find 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{it}|w_{i},x_{i},r_{i})=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Using part b, argue that 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

 is identified.
 Suggest a pooled OLS estimator.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Assume that (1) 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(u_{it}|w_{i},x_{i},c_{i},r_{i})=\sigma_{u}^{2}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

; (2) 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Cov}(u_{it},u_{is}|w_{i},x_{i},c_{i},r_{i})=0$
\end_inset

, all 
\begin_inset Formula $t\neq s$
\end_inset

; (3) 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(c_{i}|w_{i},x_{i},r_{i})=\sigma_{a}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 How would you efficiently estiamte 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

 (along with 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{0}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{1}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{2}$
\end_inset

)? [Hint: It might be helpful to write 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}=\delta_{0}+\delta_{1}r_{i}+\bar{x}_{i}\delta_{2}+a_{i}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $E(a_{i}|w_{i},x_{i},r_{i})=0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(a_{i}|w_{i},x_{i},r_{i})=\sigma_{a}^{2}]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Solution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Part a.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If we are only interested in estimating 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

, then we can perform an FE estimation on (29).
 First, we stack the equations over 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 to get
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}=\gamma w_{i}j_{T}+x_{i}\beta+c_{i}j_{T}+u_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{T}$
\end_inset

 is still the 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of ones and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{i}=\left[\begin{array}{c}
x_{i1}^{\prime}\\
\vdots\\
x_{iT}^{\prime}
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset

Next, we use the fixed effects transformation to obtain
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\ddot{y}_{i}=\ddot{x}_{i}\beta+\ddot{u}_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{y}_{i}=Q_{T}y_{i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{x}_{i}=Q_{T}x_{i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{u}_{i}=Q_{T}u_{i}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{T}=I_{T}-j_{T}(j_{T}^{\prime}j_{T})^{-1}j_{T}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 By Assumption (30), 
\begin_inset Formula $E(\ddot{u}_{it}|x_{i})=E(u_{it}|x_{i})-E(\bar{u}_{i}|x)=0$
\end_inset

, which in turns implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(\ddot{u}_{it}|\ddot{x}_{i1},\ldots,\ddot{x}_{iT})=0$
\end_inset

 since 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{x}_{it}$
\end_inset

 is just a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(\ddot{x}_{it}\ddot{u}_{it})=0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

, and if we also assume a standard rank condition, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 will be identified.
 Therefore, we can perform pooled OLS estimation on equation (32), and we
 obtain the fixed effects estimator
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\beta}_{FE}=\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\ddot{x}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{x}_{i}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\ddot{x}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{y}_{i}\right)
\]

\end_inset

which is a consistent estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Part b.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

 be a time constant proxy for 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 such that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(c_{i}|r_{i},w_{i},x_{i})=E(c_{i}|r_{i},x_{i})=\delta_{0}+\delta_{1}r_{i}+\bar{x}_{i}\delta_{2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The key assumption is that, once we condition on 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

 is not partially related to 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Furthermore, we assume that assume 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{it}|w_{i},x_{i},c_{i},r_{i})=0$
\end_inset

.
 We need to find 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left(y_{it}|w_{i},x_{i},r_{i}\right)$
\end_inset

.
 Consider equation (29)
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{it}=\gamma w_{i}+x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}+u_{it}
\]

\end_inset

Taking expectation on both sides conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

 we obtain
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E\left(y_{it}|w_{i},x_{i},r_{i}\right)=\gamma w_{i}+x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+E\left(c_{i}|w_{i},x_{i},r_{i}\right)+E\left(u_{it}|w_{i},x_{i},r_{i}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Plugging 
\begin_inset Formula $ $
\end_inset

(33) into (34) we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E\left(y_{it}|w_{i},x_{i},r_{i}\right)=\gamma w_{i}+x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+\delta_{0}+\delta_{1}r_{i}+\bar{x}_{i}\delta_{2}+E\left(u_{it}|w_{i},x_{i},r_{i}\right)
\]

\end_inset

By the law of iterated expectations, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{it}|w_{i},x_{i},r_{i})=E\left[E(u_{it}|w_{i},x_{i},c_{i},r_{i})|w_{i},x_{i},r_{i}\right]$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{it}|w_{i},x_{i},c_{i},r_{i})=0$
\end_inset

 by assumption, then 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{it}|w_{i},x_{i},r_{i})=0$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E\left(y_{it}|w_{i},x_{i},r_{i}\right)=\gamma w_{i}+x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+\delta_{0}+\delta_{1}r_{i}+\bar{x}_{i}\delta_{2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Part c.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
From part (b), we can rewrite the structural model (35) in error form as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{it}=\gamma w_{i}+x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+\delta_{0}+\delta_{1}r_{i}+\bar{x}_{i}\delta_{2}+e_{it}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(e_{it}|w_{i},x_{i},r_{i})=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

by definition.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{it}^{\prime}\equiv\left[w_{i},x_{it}^{\prime},1,r_{i},\bar{x}_{i}\right]$
\end_inset

, which has dimension 
\begin_inset Formula $1\times(K+4)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $ $
\end_inset

and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Upsilon\equiv[\gamma,\beta,\delta_{0},\delta_{1},\delta_{2}]^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, and after stacking over 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

, rewrite (36) as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}=Z_{i}\Upsilon+e_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Z_{i}=\left[\begin{array}{c}
Z_{i1}^{\prime}\\
\vdots\\
Z_{iT}^{\prime}
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset

Equation (37) is a sufficient for 
\begin_inset Formula $E(Z_{i}^{\prime}e_{i})=0$
\end_inset

.
 We also assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{rank}\ E(Z_{i}^{\prime}Z_{i})=(K+4)$
\end_inset

.
 Under these two conditions, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Upsilon$
\end_inset

 is identified, and we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
E(Z_{i}^{\prime}e_{i}) & = & 0\nonumber \\
E\left[Z_{i}^{\prime}(y_{i}-Z_{i}\Upsilon)\right] & = & 0\nonumber \\
E(Z_{i}^{\prime}Z_{i})\Upsilon & = & E(Z_{i}^{\prime}y_{i})\nonumber \\
\Upsilon & = & \left[E(Z_{i}^{\prime}Z_{i})\right]^{-1}E(Z_{i}^{\prime}y_{i})
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

The analogy principle suggests that we estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\Upsilon$
\end_inset

 by the sample analogue of equation (39).
 Thus, we obtain the pooled OLS estimator
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\hat{\Upsilon}_{POLS} & = & \left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}Z_{i}^{\prime}Z_{i}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}Z_{i}^{\prime}y_{i}\right)\\
 & = & \left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}Z_{it}Z_{it}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}Z_{it}y_{it}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

which is a consitent estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Upsilon$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

 is identified.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Part d.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We can write rewrite equation (29) as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{it}=\gamma w_{i}+x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+\delta_{0}+\delta_{1}r_{i}+\bar{x}_{i}\delta_{2}+a_{i}+u_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}=\delta_{0}+\delta_{1}r_{i}+\bar{x}_{i}\delta_{2}+a_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Note, it is assumed that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{it}|w_{i},x_{i},c_{i})=0,\ t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

.
 We also assume now that (1) 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(u_{it}|w_{i},x_{i},c_{i},r_{i})=\sigma_{u}^{2}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

; (2) 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Cov}(u_{it},u_{is}|w_{i},x_{i},c_{i},r_{i})=0$
\end_inset

, all 
\begin_inset Formula $t\neq s$
\end_inset

; (3) 
\begin_inset Formula $E(a_{i}|w_{i},x_{i},r_{i})=0$
\end_inset

; and (4) 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(a_{i}|w_{i},x_{i},r_{i})=\sigma_{a}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Keeping the same definitions as before and stacking over the 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 equations, we can rewrite equation (40) as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}=Z_{i}\Upsilon+v_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}=a_{i}j_{T}+u_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Under our given assumptions, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(v_{it}|Z_{i})=E(u_{it}+a_{i}|Z_{i})=0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

, so the regressors are strictly exogenous.
 In addition, we have that the variance matrix of 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 is constant,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(v_{i}v_{i}^{\prime}|z_{i})=E(v_{i}v_{i}^{\prime})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and that the variance matrix of 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 has the random effects structure.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Omega\equiv E(v_{i}v_{i}^{\prime})=\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
\sigma_{u}^{2}+\sigma_{a}^{2} &  & \sigma_{a}^{2}\\
 & \ddots\\
\sigma_{a}^{2} &  & \sigma_{u}^{2}+\sigma_{a}^{2}
\end{array}\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

We can write the matrix (43) as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Omega=\sigma_{u}^{2}I_{T}+\sigma_{a}^{2}j_{T}j_{T}^{\prime}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 only depends on two parameters, 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{u}^{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{a}^{2}$
\end_inset

, regardless of the size of 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

.
 Define 
